.TH "DEDB DOSEMUCONF" "1" "2026-01-01" "0.0.16" "dedb dosemuconf Manual"
.SH NAME
dedb\-dosemuconf \- Show the converted DOSEMU2 output, from dosbox.conf(s) or a game.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dedb dosemuconf
[OPTIONS] SOURCES...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Show the converted DOSEMU2 output, from dosbox.conf(s) or a game.
.PP
SOURCES is one or more dosbox.conf paths, or a single downloaded game
('gog:<id>' / 'archive:<id>', or a bare id with --backend). With none
of --conf/--userhook/--issues given, dosemu.conf and userhook.bat are
both shown. A multi-profile GOG game with no --profile shows one
[label] block per profile; --issues then reflects the default profile.
--cmdline instead prints the whole `dosemu` command (needs a game).
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-b,\fP \-\-backend SCHEME
Read SOURCES as bare game ids for this backend, rather than dosbox.conf paths.
.TP
\fB\-\-profile\fP TEXT
Launch profile, for a gog game.
.TP
\fB\-\-conf\fP
Show only dosemu.conf.
.TP
\fB\-\-userhook\fP
Show only userhook.bat.
.TP
\fB\-i,\fP \-\-issues
Show the [issues] block - what DOSEMU2 can't run as-is (same as `dosboxconf --issues`).
.TP
\fB\-v,\fP \-\-verbose
With --issues, also show each autoexec line and what it is rewritten to.
.TP
\fB\-\-cmdline\fP
Print the `dosemu` command `dedb run --dosemu` would run, and stop (game only).
